A held pointer button keeps its implicit grab after the pointer leaves.

Wayland gives the surface under a pressed button an implicit grab: the
pointer can travel anywhere, the surface still owns it, and the release
comes back to that surface. gpui read `wl_pointer.leave` as the end of
the interaction and cleared both `button_pressed` and the focused
window, so a drag that crossed the window edge lost its button state
mid-flight and the release it came back for had nowhere to go. Leave now
returns early while a button is held, and the release is what tears the
focus down, after the MouseUp has been dispatched so a drop that landed
on the way out still runs. Motion records the pointer position before
the focused-window check, because the grab keeps delivering coordinates
past the window edge and the release needs to know where the pointer
actually was.

A compositor can also cancel the grab outright (workspace switch,
overlay, Alt-Tab) and never send the release, which would leave the
press and anything dragged with it stuck to the cursor. The next enter
recovers that by synthesizing the MouseUp itself. Where the synthesized
release lands decides whether the interrupted drag drops or cancels, so
it is read off the surface the pointer came back to, not off the enter
event. Coming back to the window that held the grab releases at that
pointer's last recorded position over the window, the spot the user was
on when the grab broke. Coming back to any other surface means the drag
walked off the window, and no position over it stands for something the
user did, so the release goes to a point outside the window's content
instead. Nothing is hovered there, so no drop handler runs and gpui
clears the drag on its own, which is what a cancel looks like from the
platform layer. Either way the enter position is never used, so a
recovered drag cannot drop wherever the pointer happened to re-enter.

